llama.cpp and Legal Machines Adaptive MoE runtime extension

The packaged local generative runtime is built from the official MIT-licensed
llama.cpp b10516 source archive (upstream tag commit b95502b).

The initial Metal routed-expert interception and compact shared-buffer pool in
the Legal Machines patch derive from the MIT-licensed experimental llama.cpp
fork at https://github.com/kisasexypantera94/llama.cpp.git, commits
d1607595e47381d889c28cace0386b31a1c3cb89 through
5e9ddf13f59e3a98e34536df071fde6c5971d431. Legal Machines rebased that work
onto b10516, fixed correctness and load-boundary defects, and added structural
inspection, safety planning, deterministic LRU, telemetry, phase accounting,
elastic cache control, lifecycle handling and product integration.

The later CPU/Metal heterogeneous provider, ggml CPU orchestration hook,
assignment scheduler integration, internal numerical verifier, exact timing and
routed-work telemetry are independent Legal Machines implementations. Related
public papers, repositories, discussions and pull requests were used as research
input only; no source from them was copied into this extension.

The MIT License in this directory applies to the covered upstream llama.cpp
and derived runtime-extension material. Legal Machines product code outside
that covered material retains its own licensing terms.
